  {
    "episode_id": 87541,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/7/87541.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, it does. And that's why we approached this from such a broad-based, diverse, and bipartisan way, because we knew that AI regulation could have the potential to be contentious. And we don't think that there's anything about AI regulation that should be partisan, but it's going to require a lot of education of people and a lot of discussion. And so I asked the speaker when we formed the Task Force not to send me a bunch of technologists. I wanted people from diverse parts of the House, lots of different policy committees, lots of different political ideologies. And I also thought it was very important that we be bipartisan. So rather than the usual practice of having more seats for members of the majority than the minority, I thought we wanted an equal number of Republicans and Democrats just to demonstrate that there could be bipartisan consensus on this topic.",
