  },
  {
    "episode_id": 87541,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/7/87541.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "A driver's license or a passport, and we require you to prove who you are when we allow you to do things where potential harms could occur. So for example, if you're going to get on an airplane, an environment where someone with malicious intent could really do damage, we're going to require you to prove that you are who you say you are. So a lot of those things can be true of AI. Lots of times it's not clear when you're talking with someone, even already with a current state of AI development, if you're talking to a real person or a bot. And in the future, it's going to be even harder to distinguish between one or the other. Also, the malicious users of AI are already using it to try to pretend that they are someone who they aren't. So for example, I have spoken with several different distraught parents who got calls purportedly from their children. in a voice that sounded just like their children and with mannerisms that sounded just like their children saying, Mom, Dad, and I'm in a jail in Tijuana, they're requiring, you know, that I pay this, please get it to me right away. They're going to hurt me or whatever it is in his appearance. I can't imagine what that's like.",
